  "interpretation": {
    "finding": "Eager make/unmake accumulators reduce already-loaded evaluation cost but lower aggregate search throughput versus concurrent fused controls at both tested widths. This implementation is not an overall runtime improvement on the registered fixture set.",
    "initialPosition16K": {
      "h4FusedNpsChangePercent": -18.13,
      "h4IncrementalNpsChangePercent": -18.79,
      "h8FusedNpsChangePercent": -17.64,
      "h8IncrementalNpsChangePercent": -12.93
    },
    "mechanismInference": "The direct evaluation loop omits FEN loading and move transitions. Incremental updates occur for all make/unmake operations, including legality probes, so cheaper repeated evaluation does not establish cheaper search. No profiling attributes the measured regression to a specific component.",
    "limitations": [
      "One CI runner, eight exposed authored positions and four paired repetitions; six of eight positions are endgames. No confidence intervals or independent device replication.",
      "Fixed configuration order was preregistered; only candidate/baseline order alternates within each configuration. Mechanism-to-mechanism timing can retain drift despite concurrent controls.",
      "Same-width fused and incremental searches have identical complete search signatures; candidate-versus-HCE search trajectories can differ. These are synthetic mechanism costs, not learned evaluator strength or Elo/time saturation.",
      "Brotli sizes depend on synthetic weights. The extra research memory page does not satisfy the unchanged production memory cap.",
      "This is a distinct study with concurrent fused controls. Earlier canonical run34919106656 remains separate; no best-result selection across runs.",
      "One eager incremental implementation was tested. It does not reject all incremental, lazy, vectorized or larger-network designs."
    ]
  },
